<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julia Ward Howe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1819">1819</a>–<a href="/years/1910">1910</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Julia Ward</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Julia Ward Howe was an American author, poet and social activist who wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-hymn-of-the-republic">Battle Hymn of the Republic</a> as new words to the song <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-browns-body">John Brown's Body</a>. She wrote them in November 1861 after visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, and meeting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a>; published in the Atlantic Monthly in February 1862, the song became one of the most popular of the Union during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>. She held that freeing the slaves was right but did not believe in racial equality. After the war she worked for pacifism and women's suffrage, co-leading the American Woman Suffrage Association with Lucy Stone from 1869, and her Mothers' Day Proclamation called on women around the world to unite for peace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julia-ward-howe.jpg" alt="Julia Ward Howe" width="307" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Author unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JuliaWardHowe_2_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Julia Ward was born in New York City on 27 May 1819, the fourth of seven children. Her father, Samuel Ward III, was a Wall Street banker and a strict Calvinist Episcopalian; her mother, the poet Julia Rush Cutler Ward, died in childbirth when Julia was five. She was educated by private tutors and at schools for young ladies until sixteen, and read widely in her eldest brother's private library, which held many books at odds with the Calvinistic view. She became a Unitarian by 1841.</p><p>In 1843 she married Samuel Gridley Howe, a physician and reformer who had founded the Perkins School for the Blind, despite an age difference of eighteen years. They had six children. The marriage was troubled: her husband disapproved of her writing and took control of her estate income, and they separated in 1852. After his death in 1876 she found that most of her money had been lost in his bad investments.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Before her marriage Howe had published essays on Goethe, Schiller and Lamartine. Her first volume of poetry, Passion-Flowers, appeared anonymously, as did her second, Words for the Hour, in 1857. She also wrote plays, among them Leonora, The World's Own and Hippolytus, and published A Trip to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> in 1860. Her poems often criticized women's roles as wives and the position of women in society.</p><p>From 1868 she became active in reform. She helped found the New England Women's Club and the New England Woman Suffrage Association, and became president of the club in 1870. She supported the final version of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifteenth-amendment">Fifteenth Amendment</a>, which gave the vote to African American men without barring discrimination against women, because she viewed Black men's suffrage as the priority. In 1871 the Massachusetts Supreme Court nullified her nomination as justice of the peace, ruling that women could not hold judicial office without the legislature's authorization. From 1872 she edited the Woman's Journal for twenty years, and in 1890 she helped found the General Federation of Women's Clubs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 28 January 1908 Howe became the first woman elected to the American Academy of Arts and Letters. She died of pneumonia on 17 October 1910 at her home, Oak Glen, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portsmouth-rhode-island">Portsmouth, Rhode Island</a>, aged 91, and is buried at Mount Auburn Cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, Massachusetts. About 4,000 people sang the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-hymn-of-the-republic">Battle Hymn of the Republic</a> at her memorial service, as it had been the custom to sing it at each of her speaking engagements.</p><p>Her children wrote a biography, published in 1916, which won the Pulitzer Prize for Biography. She entered the Songwriters Hall of Fame in 1970 and the National Women's Hall of Fame in 1998, and the U.S. Postal Service issued a 14-cent stamp in her honour in 1987.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Julia Ward Howe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Julia Ward Howe</b>, an abolitionist writer, heard troops singing the song at a review outside Washington, D.C., on Munson's Hill near Bailey's Crossroads, Virginia.</blockquote><a href="/songs/battle-hymn-of-the-republic">Battle Hymn of the Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The most famous is <b>Julia Ward Howe</b>'s &quot;Battle Hymn of the Republic&quot;, written in November 1861 when a friend asked her for good words for that stirring tune; Kimball suggests that the friend was President Abraham Lincoln.</blockquote><a href="/songs/john-browns-body">John Brown's Body</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in New York City, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portsmouth-rhode-island">Portsmouth</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.71,-74.01&amp;year=1878" aria-label="New York City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2736 710 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Julia Ward Howe"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3186 980h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3186 980h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.71,-74.01&amp;year=1878">What was here in 1878? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Mine eyes have seen the glory of the coming of the Lord: He is trampling out the vintage where the grapes of wrath are stored; He hath loosed the fateful lightning of His terrible swift sword: His truth is marching on.&#8221;</p><footer>Battle Hymn of the Republic (The Atlantic Monthly, 1862) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Atlantic_Monthly/Battle_Hymn_of_the_Republic" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In the beauty of the lilies Christ was born across the sea, With a glory in his bosom that transfigures you and me: As He died to make men holy, let us die to make men free, While God is marching on.&#8221;</p><footer>Battle Hymn of the Republic (The Atlantic Monthly, 1862) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Atlantic_Monthly/Battle_Hymn_of_the_Republic" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
